DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     uuppddaattee--pplliisstt is a helper script for the target _u_p_d_a_t_e_-_p_l_i_s_t in
     bsd.port.mk(5).

     Along with options and environment variables, uuppddaattee--pplliisstt uses the exact
     same options and arguments that would be passed to pkg_create(1).  Since
     pkg_create(1) normally takes one single actual _p_k_g_n_a_m_e, there is no ambi‐
     guity in MULTI_PACKAGES situations.

     The first set of arguments correspond to the ‘default package’.

     If PORTS_PRIVSEP is in effect, uuppddaattee--pplliisstt is run as root, but will
     switch back to FAKE_TREE_OWNER for scanning the installation directory
     and to PORTS_TREE_OWNER for writing packing-lists.

     uuppddaattee--pplliisstt may need to know the PORTSDIR location in order to scan de‐
     pendencies to trim common directories.

     The options are as follows:

     --CC _d_i_r  Use a cache directory _d_i_r for scanning dependencies.  Beware that
             this directory should then be cleaned manually if the dependen‐
             cies's packing-lists change.  But this will speed up packing-list
             regeneration for ports with lots of dependencies significantly.

     --cc _v_a_r  Variable _v_a_r may be used as a way to insert @@comment when other
             variables vanish.

     --EE _e_x_t  Write new files with _e_x_t extension instead of the default _._n_e_w.

     --ee _e_x_t  Preserve old files as _e_x_t extension instead of the default _._o_r_i_g.

     --FF      Do not try to run pkg_locate(1).

     --ff      Force pkg_locate(1) to look up every file for conflicts instead
             of just the new ones.

     --II _v_a_r  Variable _v_a_r may expand to nothing, in which case the variable
             from --cc _v_a_r can be used to prevent duplicate entries.

     --ii _v_a_r  Ignore variable _v_a_r for new substitutions.  Only existing substi‐
             tutions will be considered for the new lists.

     --jj _j_o_b_s
             Use _j_o_b_s concurrent pkg_locate(1) pipes.  Default is
             ‘hw.ncpuonline’.

     --nn      Do not move the final packing-lists in position.  Instead, leave
             the _<_f_i_l_e_>_._n_e_w files for comparison.

     --qq      Quiet mode.  Do not display status progress messages.  Note that
             this is not the opposite of verbose mode.

     --rr      Run the _f_a_k_e directory scan as root.

     --SS _v_a_r  Consider variable for substitution only at the end of paths,
             e.g., for suffixes like _._p_y_c or _._p_y_o.

     --ss _v_a_r  Consider variable for substitution only at start of paths, e.g.,
             for directories like _/_e_t_c_/_r_c_._d or _/_u_s_r_/_l_o_c_a_l.

     --vv      Verbose mode.  Explain about directories stripped from dependen‐
             cies.  Note that this is not the opposite of quiet mode.

     --ww _s_u_f_f_i_x
             Warn about suffixes such as _._o_r_i_g that (usually) should not end
             into packing-lists.

     --XX _p_a_t_h
             Exclude file at absolute _p_a_t_h from the information recorded in
             the packing-list.

     uuppddaattee--pplliisstt assumes all multi-packages live under the same installation
     directory (pkg_create(1)'s --BB option), and it will scan all paths under
     that directory and dispatch them into corresponding subpackages, using
     the following heuristics:

     ••   files that were in existing packing-list(s) will be kept in the same
         packing-list.

     ••   new files and directories will be put in the packing-list that owns
         the corresponding directory.

     ••   barring that, new files will be put into the first packing-list whose
         PREFIX fits.

     As far as possible, everything that looks like actual files will be
     sorted in alphabetical order, after variable substitutions.

     uuppddaattee--pplliisstt will warn for a lot of conditions, such as pre-formatted
     manpages, or files ending in pre-patch suffixes.  Adding a @@ccoommmmeenntt
     _i_n_t_e_n_t_i_o_n_a_l_<_r_e_a_s_o_n_> will silence the warning.  For instance:

           @man man/ja_JP.EUC/cat1/kakasi.0
           @comment intentional: mandoc doesn't handle this locale

     uuppddaattee--pplliisstt will also scan dependencies, in order to strip common direc‐
     tories.

     If the _p_k_g_l_o_c_a_t_e_d_b package is installed, uuppddaattee--pplliisstt will make use of it
     and report unregistered conflicts.  This can be disabled with --FF as it is
     time-consuming.

     Most SUBST_VARS variables will be back-substituted into the regenerated
     packing-lists, starting from the longest variable values.  uuppddaattee--pplliisstt
     tries to keep existing substitutions.  It does not add new substitutions
     on BASE_PKGPATH and BUILD_PKGPATH, it does only substitute FULLPKGNAME in
     _s_h_a_r_e_/_d_o_c_/_p_k_g_-_r_e_a_d_m_e_s and it currently does not add new substitutions for
     ARCH nor MACHINE_ARCH.

     Some packages (notoriously Python packages) create some directories op‐
     tionally based on flavors.  Options --cc and --II can be used to avoid dupli‐
     cate directory definitions.

     Specifically, a generated packing-list would contain
           lib/python${MODPY_VERSION}/site-packages/bpdb/
           lib/python${MODPY_VERSION}/site-packages/bpdb/${MODPY_PYCACHE}/
     which expands to
           lib/python3.7/site-packages/bpdb/
           lib/python3.7/site-packages/bpdb/__pycache__/
     for python3, which is fine.

     But for python2, variable ‘MODPY_PYCACHE’ will be empty, resulting in
           lib/python2.7/site-packages/bpdb/
           lib/python2.7/site-packages/bpdb/
     thus a duplicate directory, pkg_create(1) won't be happy.

     Using UPDATE_PLIST_ARGS = --cc _M_O_D_P_Y___C_O_M_M_E_N_T --II _M_O_D_P_Y___P_Y_C_A_C_H_E will result
     in injecting ‘${MODPY_COMMENT}’ wherever uuppddaattee--pplliisstt finds a duplicate
     directory by replacing ‘${MODPY_PYCACHE}’ with nothing.

     This yields
           lib/python${MODPY_VERSION}/site-packages/bpdb/
           ${MODPY_COMMENT}lib/python${MODPY_VERSION}/site-packages/bpdb/MODPY_PYCACHE}/
     and for python2 this expands to
           lib/python2.7/site-packages/bpdb/
           @comment lib/python2.7/site-packages/bpdb/
     which is exactly what we want.

     Specific items such as shared libraries or binaries will gain annotations
     and special handling, for instance LIBlibname_VERSION.

     Existing packing-lists are scanned for non-file entries, such as nneewwuusseerr,
     mmooddee, or eexxeecc markers.  Those will be inserted into the updated packing-
     lists at the most likely position.

     uuppddaattee--pplliisstt will first write files as _<_f_i_l_e_>_._n_e_w, then it will display
     which files are new and which files have changed.  If old packing-lists
     already exist, it will move old packing lists into _<_f_i_l_e_>_._o_r_i_g, then move
     the new files into position.

